.Dd November 27, 2026
.Dt ZFS-KEYSLOT 8
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm zfs-keyslot
.Nd add, change, rewrap, remove, or list the key slots of an encrypted dataset
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm zfs
.Cm keyslot
.Cm add
.Op Fl S Ar slot
.Op Fl o Ar keyformat Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl o Ar keylocation Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl o Ar pbkdf2iters Ns = Ns Ar value
.Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
.Nm zfs
.Cm keyslot
.Cm set
.Fl S Ar slot
.Fl o Ar keylocation Ns = Ns Ar value
.Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
.Nm zfs
.Cm keyslot
.Cm remove
.Fl S Ar slot
.Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
.Nm zfs
.Cm keyslot
.Cm rewrap
.Fl S Ar slot
.Op Fl o Ar keylocation Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl o Ar pbkdf2iters Ns = Ns Ar value
.Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
.Nm zfs
.Cm keyslot
.Cm list
.Op Fl Hjp
.Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An encrypted dataset is encrypted with a master key, and that master key is
stored wrapped by the wrapping key of its encryption root.
The wrapping key is derived from the key the user supplies, so a key slot holds
one wrapping of the encryption root's wrapping key.
Any slot can unlock the same dataset, while each slot has its own key material,
key format, and source, which allows one dataset to be unlocked with several
independent keys.
.Pp
Slot 1 is the primary slot.
It always exists, is created by
.Nm zfs Cm create ,
and is the wrapping that
.Nm zfs Cm change-key
replaces.
Every slot above the primary one wraps that same key, so
.Nm zfs Cm change-key
on that root is refused while they exist
.Pq see Xr zfs-load-key 8 .
To replace the secret of one of those slots instead, use
.Nm zfs Cm keyslot Cm rewrap .
.Nm zfs Cm load-key
reads slot 1 unless
.Fl S
selects another slot.
Slots 2 and above are added with
.Nm zfs Cm keyslot
and require the
.Sy keyslots
pool feature
.Pq see Xr zpool-features 7 .
Adding a slot does not change the dataset's keys or re-encrypt any data; it adds
another wrapping of the same wrapping key.
.Pp
Slots belong to the encryption root, so every verb takes the encryption root as
its target; naming a dataset below it is refused with a message naming the root.
Every verb also fails on a dataset that is not encrypted.
Adding or removing a slot requires the root's key to be loaded, so
.Sy keystatus
must be
.Sy available .
Rewrapping a slot's secret requires the key for the same reason: the loaded key
is what authorizes the operation.
Changing a slot's source and listing slots read only metadata and work on a
locked root.
.Pp
The unlock paths that have no human present read slot 1 and never try another
slot:
.Nm zfs Cm mount Fl l ,
.Nm zpool Cm import Fl l ,
.Nm zpool Cm split Fl l ,
the systemd mount generator
.Pq see Xr zfs-mount-generator 8 ,
the SysV
.Sy zfs-load-key
init script,
and the
.Fx
.Sy zfskeys
script.
Slot 1 must therefore stay loadable without interaction if one of them is
relied on, and an extra slot does not replace it.
Interactive unlock paths do use the extra slots.
The initramfs-tools boot script and the dracut hooks try each slot's stored
source first and then offer one typed secret to every
.Sy passphrase
slot;
.Sy zfsunlock
and
.Xr pam_zfs_key 8
offer the secret to every
.Sy passphrase
slot.
A recovery passphrase held only in a slot above the primary one therefore
still unlocks the dataset at boot or at login.
.Nm zfs Cm load-key Fl S Ar slot
selects a slot explicitly, and
.Fl a
and
.Fl r
apply the selection to every encryption root they reach
.Pq see Xr zfs-load-key 8 .
.Pp
Slots are part of the dataset, so a raw send
.Pq Nm zfs Cm send Fl w
carries every slot, and each slot's stored source travels with the properties
when
.Fl p
is given.
An implementation that does not support key slots ignores them without
reporting an error, so a stream from a dataset that has extra key slots must be
received on a system that supports the
.Sy keyslots
feature.
.Bl -tag -width ""
.It Xo
.Nm zfs
.Cm keyslot
.Cm add
.Op Fl S Ar slot
.Op Fl o Ar keyformat Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl o Ar keylocation Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl o Ar pbkdf2iters Ns = Ns Ar value
.Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
.Xc
Adds a key slot to the encryption root, wrapping its wrapping key under the new
slot's key.
The key is read or prompted for exactly as
.Nm zfs Cm create
does for a new dataset, and the slot's key format, salt, and iteration count are
recorded with the slot.
The number of the new slot is printed.
Slot 1 is the primary slot and cannot be created; when
.Fl S
is omitted, the lowest free slot is allocated.
.Bl -tag -width "-S"
.It Fl S Ar slot
Use the given slot number instead of allocating the lowest free one.
The value must be 2 or greater and the slot must be free.
.It Fl o Ar keyformat Ns = Ns Ar value
The format of the new slot's key: one of
.Sy raw ,
.Sy hex ,
or
.Sy passphrase .
This property is required; see
.Xr zfsprops 7 .
.It Fl o Ar keylocation Ns = Ns Ar value
The source used when this slot is loaded.
The default is
.Sy prompt .
A value of
.Sy none
is refused for encrypted datasets.
The location is stored in the
.Sy keylocation_ Ns Ar slot
property.
.It Fl o Ar pbkdf2iters Ns = Ns Ar value
The number of PBKDF2 iterations used to derive a
.Sy passphrase
slot's key; see
.Xr zfsprops 7 .
.El
.Pp
The command fails if the
.Sy keyslots
feature is not enabled, if the key is not loaded, if the dataset was created
with an encryption version that cannot hold key slots, or if the requested slot
is already in use.
.Pp
Adding a slot activates the
.Sy keyslots
pool feature.
This is one-way: once a slot has been written, a build that does not support key
slots cannot import the pool at all, not even read-only, so a rescue environment
needs an implementation that has the feature.
Removing the slot does not reverse this
.Pq see Xr zpool-features 7 .
On a root pool, regenerate the initramfs after installing a build that supports
the feature and before adding the first slot: an initramfs built from an
implementation without it cannot import the pool, so the system would not boot.
.It Xo
.Nm zfs
.Cm keyslot
.Cm set
.Fl S Ar slot
.Fl o Ar keylocation Ns = Ns Ar value
.Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
.Xc
Changes the stored source of an existing slot in place.
Nothing is re-wrapped: the slot keeps its key material, and only the location it
is loaded from changes.
Slot 1 edits the
.Sy keylocation
property; any other slot edits
.Sy keylocation_ Ns Ar slot .
The slot must exist.
.Bl -tag -width "-S"
.It Fl S Ar slot
The slot to edit.
This option is required.
.It Fl o Ar keylocation Ns = Ns Ar value
The new source.
This option is required and is the only property accepted, because a slot's key
format and iteration count are fixed when the slot is created.
.El
.Pp
The edit is authorized by the
.Sy keylocation
permission, not by
.Sy keyslot .
.It Xo
.Nm zfs
.Cm keyslot
.Cm remove
.Fl S Ar slot
.Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
.Xc
Removes a key slot.
The slot's key material and the location stored for it are deleted in the same
transaction, so the slot can no longer unlock the dataset.
A location that the dataset received from a stream is deleted as well, so no
stored source is left behind for a slot that does not exist.
Slot 1 cannot be removed, and slot 0 is not a slot.
The key must be loaded, so a recovery slot cannot be removed while the root is
locked.
.Pp
Removal is not secure erasure: like
.Nm zfs Cm change-key ,
it does not overwrite the previous wrapping on disk, so that wrapping may remain
recoverable by forensic analysis.
See the warning in
.Xr zfs-load-key 8
for what to do when a key is compromised.
.It Xo
.Nm zfs
.Cm keyslot
.Cm rewrap
.Fl S Ar slot
.Op Fl o Ar keylocation Ns = Ns Ar value
.Op Fl o Ar pbkdf2iters Ns = Ns Ar value
.Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
.Xc
Replaces the secret that unlocks an existing slot.
The encryption root's wrapping key is wrapped under a key derived from the new
secret, and the slot is rewritten in one transaction, so the slot number and the
slot's key format are kept, and the wrapping key, slot 1, and every other slot
are unchanged.
The old secret is not asked for, and the loaded key is what authorizes the
operation, so a slot whose secret is lost can be rewrapped without being removed
first.
.Bl -tag -width "-S"
.It Fl S Ar slot
The slot to rewrap.
This option is required, and the slot must exist.
Slot 0 is not a slot, and slot 1 cannot be rewrapped: its entries wrap the
master key, which
.Nm zfs Cm change-key
replaces.
.It Fl o Ar keylocation Ns = Ns Ar value
The source of the new secret, which is stored for the slot as well.
The default is
.Sy prompt .
When the option is omitted the new secret comes from a prompt and the stored
source becomes
.Sy prompt ,
so a slot that must stay file-backed has to name that file here, even when the
file is unchanged.
.It Fl o Ar pbkdf2iters Ns = Ns Ar value
The number of PBKDF2 iterations used to derive a
.Sy passphrase
slot's new key.
If the option is not given the slot's current count is preserved, so a rewrap
changes the secret without changing the slot's work factor.
Give this option to raise or lower the count deliberately.
.El
.Pp
A slot's key format is fixed when the slot is created, so
.Fl o Ar keyformat
is refused.
.Fl o Ar keylocation
and
.Fl o Ar pbkdf2iters
are the only properties accepted, and a location of
.Sy none
is refused as it is for
.Nm zfs Cm keyslot Cm add .
.Pp
The command fails if the
.Sy keyslots
feature is not enabled, if the key is not loaded, if the slot does not exist, or
if the slot's stored key format cannot be used, in which case the slot is
removed and added again instead.
.Pp
A rewrap protects against future use of a compromised secret, but it is not
secure erasure: the previous wrapping is not overwritten on disk.
.It Xo
.Nm zfs
.Cm keyslot
.Cm list
.Op Fl Hjp
.Ar filesystem Ns | Ns Ar volume
.Xc
Prints one row per slot, slot 1 included, sorted by slot number: the slot
number, key format, PBKDF2 salt, PBKDF2 iteration count, and the stored
location.
A slot with no stored location is shown with a dash.
Only metadata is read, so the command works on a locked root and never prints
key material.
.Bl -tag -width "-H"
.It Fl H
Do not print a header line.
.It Fl j
Print the rows as JSON, as a
.Sy keyslots
object keyed by slot number.
.It Fl p
Print numeric values; the key format is then printed as the numeric value of the
.Sy keyformat
property.
.El
.El
.Ss Delegation
.Nm zfs Cm keyslot Cm add ,
.Nm zfs Cm keyslot Cm rewrap ,
and
.Nm zfs Cm keyslot Cm remove
require the
.Sy keyslot
permission, which must be granted on the encryption root.
.Nm zfs Cm keyslot Cm set
requires the
.Sy keylocation
permission, and
.Nm zfs Cm keyslot Cm list
is not gated, because it prints only the slot metadata that
.Nm zfs Cm get
already reports.
See
.Xr zfs-allow 8 .
.Pp
A user granted
.Sy keyslot
can add a slot using key material of their own choosing, or replace the secret
of an existing slot, while the root is unlocked, and revoking the permission
does not undo either; the slot has to be listed, and removed, explicitly.
Grant
.Sy keyslot
with the same care as
.Sy load-key .
.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zfsprops 7 ,
.Xr zpool-features 7 ,
.Xr zfs-allow 8 ,
.Xr zfs-create 8 ,
.Xr zfs-load-key 8
